  "party_positions": [
    {
      "claims": [
        {
          "attribution": "Petitioner",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "9c53cd22348e0d45e776db634e3e615fbf548a5790dd49b0ab2ce0468e32b008",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-9c53cd22348e-0000-0000"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 3,
                "start": 3
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Petitioner, antisubrogation laws relate to benefits and coverage, as the Court previously held in FMC v. Holliday."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Petitioner",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "9c53cd22348e0d45e776db634e3e615fbf548a5790dd49b0ab2ce0468e32b008",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-9c53cd22348e-0000-0000"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 3,
                "start": 3
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Petitioner, if the statutory language is ambiguous, the OPM notice\u2011and\u2011comment regulation resolves the issue in favor of preemption."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Petitioner",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "9c53cd22348e0d45e776db634e3e615fbf548a5790dd49b0ab2ce0468e32b008",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-9c53cd22348e-0000-0000"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 3,
                "start": 3
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Petitioner, there is no constitutional infirmity in the Missouri rule."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Petitioner (Mr. Tripp)",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "9c53cd22348e0d45e776db634e3e615fbf548a5790dd49b0ab2ce0468e32b008",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-9c53cd22348e-0001-0000"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 20,
                "start": 20
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Petitioner (Mr. Tripp), according to Petitioner (Mr. Tripp), even without Chevron deference, the petitioner would win because FEHBA contains an express\u2011preemption provision covering federal benefits for federal employees."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Petitioner (Mr. Tripp)",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "9c53cd22348e0d45e776db634e3e615fbf548a5790dd49b0ab2ce0468e32b008",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-9c53cd22348e-0001-0001"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 20,
                "start": 20
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Petitioner (Mr. Tripp), according to Petitioner (Mr. Tripp), if Missouri can prohibit subrogation, federal workers in other states would bear the cost of benefit payments they cannot retain, which exceeds traditional state authority."
        }
      ],
      "heading": "Petitioner Position",
      "status": "complete",
      "summary": null,
      "summary_citations": []
    },
    {
      "claims": [
        {
          "attribution": "Respondent (Mr. Wessler)",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "9c53cd22348e0d45e776db634e3e615fbf548a5790dd49b0ab2ce0468e32b008",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-9c53cd22348e-0001-0004"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 30,
                "start": 30
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Respondent (Mr. Wessler), according to Respondent (Mr. Wessler), congress intended the statute not to disturb otherwise applicable state insurance laws."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Mr. Wessler",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "9c53cd22348e0d45e776db634e3e615fbf548a5790dd49b0ab2ce0468e32b008",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-9c53cd22348e-0002-0003"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 38,
                "start": 38
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Mr. Wessler, a better approach is to point to a statute that expressly preempts state law."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Mr. Wessler",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "9c53cd22348e0d45e776db634e3e615fbf548a5790dd49b0ab2ce0468e32b008",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-9c53cd22348e-0002-0004"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 39,
                "start": 39
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Mr. Wessler, the terms of the contracts determine the scope of preemption, and those terms were not known by Congress when the law was enacted."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Mr. Wessler",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "9c53cd22348e0d45e776db634e3e615fbf548a5790dd49b0ab2ce0468e32b008",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-9c53cd22348e-0002-0005"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 40,
                "start": 40
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Mr. Wessler, eRISA includes substantive provisions that dictate which state laws are displaced by federal law."
        }
      ],
